 | `test_batched_stills.py` (two tests) | Project checkpoints must capture their own authoring cursor and follow final timed-context rescaling before batched rendering. These contracts span Scene exports, Project scene execution, and timeline events; regressions in any of those can silently select the wrong frame. Only the author-then-render and relative-context-time checks are marked. They use a stub renderer; the real pixel comparisons remain feature tests. |
+| `test_batch_preparation_devices.py` (two tests) | Only the render/encoder cleanup-order checks: `test_render_reclaims_arena_before_deferred_pressure_reset` and `test_video_defers_pressure_cleanup_until_encoder_and_frame_locals_are_gone`. They hold the contract between the render loop, prefetch-worker shutdown, scene GC protection, the memory arena, and deferred compiler resets. Changing any of those can reclaim a still-live buffer or retain an entire render arena. Stub renderers and memory hooks; no real render. |
+| `test_manim_compat_movement.py` (one test, parametrized for Axes and Star) | Only `test_translation_replays_existing_rows_without_a_geometry_morph`. Changes to Mob translation, timeline recording/replay, or timed contexts can desynchronize the Algan geometry and its backing Manim object, or rebuild the hierarchy during a simple move. It checks both representations at several timeline positions and preserves descendant identity; no render. The remaining compatibility feature tests stay out. |
+| `test_taichi_runtime_config.py` (one test) | Only `test_cached_cuda_reset_retains_memory_pressure_fallbacks`. Windows physical/commit-memory telemetry from `memory_utils`, compiler-specialization tracking, and render-lifetime guards jointly determine when a cached CUDA runtime can be retained. Changes outside runtime configuration can otherwise discard reusable kernels under ordinary RAM pressure or suppress a necessary reset near the commit limit. Mocked telemetry and compiler hooks; no GPU initialization or render. |
 | `tests/fast/test_fast_render.py` | One real scene, rendered and compared pixel-wise. The only thing in the loop that can see a renderer regression, and most of its wall clock. |
